{"type": "FeatureCollection", "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "San Pedro, Los Angeles and Salt Lake Railroad", "line": "San Pedro, Los Angeles and Salt Lake: Salt Lake Route", "id": "splasl-los-angeles-salt-lake-city", "color": "#F2A900", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 75, "speed_source": "estimated/blended: the Los Angeles Limited's 68-74 hour Chicago-LA schedule at opening implies roughly 55-65 km/h on this segment, rising to modern-era speeds (~100+ km/h) for the streamlined City of Los Angeles and Amtrak's Desert Wind; unsourced blend", "segments": [{"start": 1905, "end": 1916, "headway_min": 480, "note": "Incorporated in Utah March 21, 1901 by Montana Senator William A. Clark as the San Pedro, Los Angeles and Salt Lake Railroad, racing Union Pacific's Oregon Short Line (building south from Salt Lake City) to control the route. The two sides resolved their conflict in July 1903 when Harriman's OSL bought a 50% stake. Through track was completed in May 1905; the town of Las Vegas was founded at the railroad's division point, its lots auctioned May 15, 1905. The flagship Los Angeles Limited began running Chicago-Los Angeles (jointly with the Chicago & North Western and Union Pacific) on Dec 17, 1905, on a 68-74 hour schedule via Cheyenne, Ogden, Salt Lake City, Las Vegas, and San Bernardino. Frequency is estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles_and_Salt_Lake_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1916, "end": 1921, "headway_min": 480, "note": "Renamed the Los Angeles and Salt Lake Railroad on Aug 16, 1916, though still half-owned by Clark. Service continued much as before; frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles_and_Salt_Lake_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1921, "end": 1936, "headway_min": 360, "note": "Union Pacific bought out Clark's remaining 50% stake on April 27, 1921, giving it full control (formally leased into unified UP operations Jan 1, 1936). The Los Angeles Limited remained UP's flagship on this corridor through the 1920s; a 63-hour premium schedule ran briefly from November 1926 to June 1929. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles_and_Salt_Lake_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1936, "end": 1954, "headway_min": 240, "note": "The streamlined City of Los Angeles entered service May 15, 1936, taking over as UP's premier Chicago-LA train and eclipsing the older, heavyweight Los Angeles Limited, which continued as a secondary train (going daily again after the City of LA did in 1947). Aggregate frequency across both trains estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles_and_Salt_Lake_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1954, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 720, "note": "The Los Angeles Limited was replaced by the streamlined, dieselized Los Angeles Challenger on Jan 10, 1954, running alongside the City of Los Angeles (39h45m Chicago-LA by the late 1960s). Ridership declined through the 1950s-60s in line with the national trend; the City of Los Angeles made its final run for Union Pacific April 30 - May 2, 1971, the last regular passenger service to Las Vegas before Amtrak. Frequency for this era estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles_and_Salt_Lake_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1979, "end": 1997, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Amtrak dropped this corridor entirely on May 1, 1971 (aside from brief 1976 charter/excursion service, including a four-month weekend-only Las Vegas Limited). Regular service returned Sept 1979 with the Desert Wind, running Chicago-Salt Lake City-Las Vegas-Los Angeles (combined with the California Zephyr east of Salt Lake City). Amtrak discontinued the Desert Wind May 12, 1997 citing high costs and weak ridership, replacing it with a Los Angeles-Las Vegas Thruway bus; no passenger train has served this corridor since. Today it remains an active Union Pacific freight mainline (the Los Angeles, Cima, Caliente, Lynndyl, and Salt Lake Subdivisions).", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles_and_Salt_Lake_Railroad"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-113.7481, 37.86488], "to": [-113.53747, 37.9417], "chord_mi": 12.8, "traced_mi": 12.66, "snap_m": [1, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Caliente Subdivision (12.66 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-114.35633, 37.54985], "to": [-114.17451, 37.54741], "chord_mi": 10.06, "traced_mi": 13.15, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Calient